{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Parent\n\nEpic `77e3-28e2-31ec-47ec`, deliverable 6, authorized at full scope on 2026-11-02.\n\n## Depends on `fe94-2e22-ea9e-46f7`\n\nThe tidying janitor's review criterion is \"net LOC and non-positive mechanism delta\", and\nthe mechanism-delta half is what the net-mechanism criterion story formalizes. Writing\nthis skill first would invent a second, divergent definition of the same measure.\n\n## The gap\n\nThe janitor's PREMISE is sound: a deliberate pause between features to buy back\noptionality. Its objective function contradicts the premise in three architectural ways.\n\n1. **The Phase 2 floor selects for defects, not complexity.** Validity 0.75, impact 0.5 and\n   reachable harm find bugs, and defect remediation adds mechanism more often than\n   it removes it. The 2026-08-29 run's 37 items were mostly ADDITIONS: oracles, health\n   commands, drift checks, metrics, pinning. Its removals came from the operator manually\n   expanding scope, not from the skill.\n\n2. **\"Never edits code\" turns tidying into ticket generation.** 20 to 37 tickets per run\n   enter the same pipeline that produces the complexity, with no net-LOC or net-mechanism\n   budget. Eight identical \"Restore headroom under the 800-LOC cap\" epics exist.\n\n3. **Plans are approved against a frozen snapshot while `main` moves.** The 2026-08-29\n   approvals ran to 09-02 and the dead-code validation recensused 98 commits later, so\n   cites were stale by claim time.\n\nThis is self-defeating, not merely inefficient: janitor remediation AUTHORED artifacts\nthis program then deleted. RP-04's `REBAR_RECONCILER_WRITE_FACADE` switch, its 8\n`*_seam_clean_heldout.py` tests and config-ownership gate; RP-03's `_call_with_retry` and\n`CREATE_ROUTE` toggle; the 08-06 run's MCP drift gate and complexity-baseline CI lock; the\n08-29 run's alias tombstones (landed 09-04, deleted 3 days later) and the parity manifest\nthat followed its rejection of shared MCP contracts (09-01, deleted 6 days later).\n\n## The change — modify, not discard\n\nKEEP: discovery, blind verification, prior-decision checking, one-at-a-time approval.\nThose four are what make the skill trustworthy and none of them is implicated.\n\nSPLIT into two skills:\n\n- a **tidying janitor** (`examples/agent-skills/rebar-tidy/`) whose only admissible\n  outputs are deletions and collapses, judged\n  at review by net LOC and non-positive mechanism delta, run between features\n- a **defect audit** — the current skill, substantially unchanged — run less often and\n  NOT called cleanup\n\nThen change the tidying janitor's contract:\n\n- a run is capped at 5 tickets, each landable in one session\n- each ticket it files states the deletion it will make, measured at the discovery SHA:\n  lines removed, and mechanisms retired by kind and name (the seven kinds the ratchet\n  counts, `scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py`)\n- the run itself sums those declared deltas before filing, and a run whose sum is not\n  negative files nothing and says so — that sum, not tickets filed, is the run's result\n- the corrective loop runs through the tracker the skill already reads for prior\n  decisions: a closed tidying ticket whose merged diff removed less than it declared\n  records the shortfall, and a change class that twice failed to net-reduce is not\n  re-proposed\n- it files no gates; a finding that wants a gate becomes an idea for the operator\n- discovery is against a SHA, with approval and ticketization inside 24 hours or the plan\n  is discarded\n- no per-file mechanical sweeps; a condensation is one atomic change or is not done\n\nThe last one has its own evidence: the comment-condensation sweep the janitor seeded ran\nas roughly 1,700 per-file tasks whose commits invalidated cites in 6 breaking-pass stories\nand whose worktrees held the host's gate slots.\n\n## Explicitly NOT attributed to the janitor\n\nThe duplicate-ticket incident (tier-2 replay harness) and the mechanism ratchet (Track I\nepic) were not janitor output, so the split is not justified by them.\n\n## Provider neutrality\n\nrebar must support a wide variety of providers and models. Both skills are prompt-shaped\nand must not assume a vendor's model, tool-calling syntax, or harness.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\nEach criterion below is met or not met by reading the two skill definitions, except\nwhere a command or test is named.\n\n- [ ] Two skills exist where one did: a tidying janitor and a defect audit.\n- [ ] The tidying janitor's admissible outputs are deletions and collapses only, stated\n      explicitly.\n- [ ] It declares a per-run ticket cap of 5.\n- [ ] Its success metric is the summed declared delta of the tickets a run files, computed\n      by the run before filing, and NOT tickets filed.\n- [ ] It states that a run whose summed delta is not negative files nothing.\n- [ ] It names the actor and loop that correct a shortfall: the next run reads closed\n      tidying tickets and does not re-propose a class that twice failed to net-reduce.\n- [ ] It states that it files no gates and that a gate-wanting finding becomes an idea.\n- [ ] It states that discovery is against a SHA with a 24-hour approval-and-ticketization\n      window after which the plan is discarded.\n- [ ] It states that a condensation is one atomic change, prohibiting per-file mechanical\n      sweeps.\n- [ ] The defect audit retains discovery, blind verification, prior-decision checking and\n      one-at-a-time approval, proving the split dropped none of the parts that worked.\n- [ ] The defect audit is not named or described as cleanup.\n- [ ] Neither skill names a specific model or vendor. Verified by a test.\n\n## Plan grounding\n\nMeasured against `origin/main` at `e642101fb56`. The skill is 7 files, 1,014 lines;\n`examples/agent-skills/rebar-janitor/SKILL.md` is 128 lines.\n\n**\"Never edits code\"** is stated three times and all three move with the split:\n`SKILL.md:14` (the frontmatter a harness reads to decide whether to invoke), `:68` (the\nrule), `:127` (the closing restatement).\n\n**The Phase-2 floor** is authoritative in `phases/verification.md`, not `SKILL.md` (which\nonly summarises it at `:119`). The DROP block `:159-163` reads an inaccurate-citation veto\n(`:159`), `validity < 0.75` (`:161`), `impact < 0.5` (`:162`). Reachable harm is `:42`\n(`harm_reachable`, demonstration required), and `:49` RECLASSIFIES a no-reachable-harm\nfinding as `HARDENING` rather than dropping it — a distinction the split inherits.\n\n**A new skill directory is auto-discovered by a CI gate.** `scripts/check_skill_frontmatter.py`\nglobs `**/SKILL.md` under `examples/agent-skills` (`find_skill_files`, `:51`; the glob\nitself is `:55`), so the\ntidying janitor's `SKILL.md` is validated the moment it exists, with no registration step.\nIts frontmatter must satisfy `:45-47`: `description` required, non-empty, <= 1024\ncharacters and containing no angle brackets; `name` optional but, when present, <= 64\ncharacters matching `^[a-z0-9]+(-[a-z0-9]+)*$`. A non-conforming skill is silently dropped\nby Copilot CLI, which is why this is a gate and not a convention.\n\n**One existing test hard-codes the janitor's path.**\n`tests/unit/test_janitor_concern_schema.py:21` builds\n`examples/agent-skills/rebar-janitor/phases/discovery.md` as a literal and asserts a\nconcern list contiguous from one (`:87`) whose count matches the schema (`:97`). The plan\nkeeps that directory, so the test is untouched; a rename would have to move it.\n\n`tests/unit/test_skill_shared_sync.py:14` records that `rebar-janitor` is NOT a consumer\nof `shared/test-design.md`. Neither skill becomes one, so its consumer list is unchanged.\n\n- [ ] `python scripts/check_skill_frontmatter.py` exits 0 with both skills present.\n- [ ] `tests/unit/test_janitor_concern_schema.py` passes unchanged, proving the defect\n      audit kept the concern list contiguous and schema-aligned.\n- [ ] `tests/unit/test_skill_shared_sync.py` passes unchanged, proving neither skill was\n      made a consumer of the shared test-design file.\n"}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1793688445880913001,"uuid":"dc9b32b7-95de-483a-b4eb-1f46af6b7f29"}